  "id": "kibanda-lunch",
  "uuid": "f69e4cbe-5a64-55a2-9599-39ac6f115d59",
  "title": "Lunch at the Kibanda",
  "category": "food-drink",
  "difficulty": "medium",
  "level": 5,
  "order": 505,
  "theme": "food",
  "culturalNote": "A kibanda (or \"kibanda cha chakula\") is a roadside food stall: plastic chairs, a hot plate, and the best-value lunch in Nairobi — chapati, beans (maharagwe), ugali with meat or sukuma. Tea comes already sweetened unless you say otherwise. To order, Kenyans usually say \"Niletee…\" (bring me…) or the softer \"Naweza kupata…?\" (can I get…?). \"Karibu chakula\" is the polite thing to say when food arrives — lit. \"welcome to the food\".",
  "startingPoint": "Lunchtime. You sit down at a kibanda near the office.",
